 "text": "Based on my knowledge of medical literature and clinical studies, I can provide an answer to this biomedical research question.\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsSince web search isn't available, I'll provide an answer based on my existing medical knowledge.\n\nPerforated appendicitis (particularly when there is a rupture and spillage of infectious material into the abdominal cavity) can lead to pelvic inflammatory disease and adhesions involving the fallopian tubes. These adhesions and inflammation can potentially increase the risk of tubal infertility and ectopic pregnancy by:\n\n1. Damaging the tubal epithelium and cilia\n2. Causing scarring and narrowing of the tubal lumen\n3. Creating mechanical obstructions\n4. Interfering with normal ovum pickup and transport\n\nHowever, the association is not as strong or well-established as with other causes of pelvic inflammatory disease (like chlamydia or gonorrhea). Modern early surgical intervention for appendicitis has reduced this risk compared to historical cases.\n\n```json\n{\"answer\": \"maybe\", \"confidence\": 65}\n```",
